III. I
NCORPORATING ETC W ITH THE MAC L AYER
In this section, we present the design and implemen-
tation of three innovative TDMA-based MAC protocolswhich enable the deployment of TTC, PETC, PSDETC, and
PADETC approaches accordingl y: control-TDMA (C-TDMA),
synchronous decentralized C-TDMA (SDC-TDMA), andasynchronous decentralized C-TDMA (ADC-TDMA). The
main beneﬁts of these ETC-speciﬁc MAC protocols are:
the optimization of communications by fully exploiting thebehavior and needs of ETC; the minimization of actuator node
listening through duty cycling; and the off-load of the local
controller node by allowing only one node to communicate
with a base station per time.
For the proposed TDMA-based communication schemes,
we assume a city-scale CPS network infrastructure, such as
in Fig. 1, which represents a smart water network. In this
architecture, the sensors and actua tors are divided into clusters.
