1https://www.lora-alliance.org/factor S,log2(S)bits deﬁne f0,” i.e., the initial frequency shift,
but there is no explanation of how this is done.
Actually the LoRa modulation is often referred to as a “chirp
modulation” [8]–[10]. A close inspection of LoRa reveals that
the information bearing element is the frequency shift at the
beginning of the symbol and the chirp is similar to a kind of a
carrier. For this reason, in our opinion, LoRa is better described
as a Frequency Shift Chirp Modulation (FSCM).
The rest of the paper is organised as follows. In Section II,
we provide a description of the modulation process and identify
the orthogonal signals basis characterising the modulation; in
Section III, we provide a description of the optimum demod-
ulator and an efﬁcient implementation of it making use of the
Fast Fourier Transform; in Section IV, we provide the results
of some computer simulations experiments on the link level
performances, comparing as well the FSCM modulation with
an frequency-shift keying (FSK) modulation with the same car-
dinality. Finally in Section V, we draw the conclusions of the
paper.
